Each provided .reg contains a REPLACE_ME_FOR_UTV_HOME (or REPLACE_ME_FOR_RV_HOME) placeholder that must be replaced with the location of the application.
Also included is a .bat version of each .reg file. Each .bat file sets the registry entry and automatically configures paths with the location of the application.

The "standard" windows registry files here are:

    openwith.reg / openwith.reg.bat

        Launch UTV when double-clicking on media files.

    rvlink.reg / rvlink.reg.bat

        Register UTV as the handler for "utvlink://" and "rvlink://" custom protocols.  Docs on the protocol here:

            https://github.com/OpenUTV/utv/blob/main/docs/rv-manuals/rv-user-manual/rv-user-manual-chapter-c.md


The "utvpush" / "rvpush" files are alternates to support different workflows.  UTVPUSH (rvpush)
is an executable we provide that allows you to communicate with a running UTV
from the command line.  Docs here:

    https://github.com/OpenUTV/utv/blob/main/docs/rv-manuals/rv-user-manual/rv-user-manual-chapter-eighteen.md

It is a general utility, but it is used here in the .reg files to avoid UTV
startup time when loading media by using a running UTV if one is available.

In particular:

    rvlink_rvpush.reg / rvlink_rvpush.reg.bat

        This one uses UTVPUSH to handle URLs (both utvlink:// and rvlink://), i.e. the first one will open a new
        UTV (tagged as the URL handler) and if that UTV is still running when
        next you click on a URL, that will be handled by the running UTV instead
        of a new one.

    openwith_rvpush_set.reg / openwith_rvpush_set.reg.bat

        Similar to the above, but for the "double click on a file" workflow.

    openwith_rvpush_merge.reg / openwith_rvpush_merge.reg.bat
    
        This uses the "merge" action instead of the "set" action for UTVPUSH,
        which means that double-clicking on a second file after you have
        already opened one will merge that media into the running UTV instead of
        replacing the previous media.
